---
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro';
import ConceptImage from '../../components/ConceptImage.astro';
import CTA from '../../components/CTA.astro';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Investment Property Renovations in North Alabama"
  description="Plan investment property renovations in North Alabama with clear unit priorities, durable material choices, and owner visibility. Start your Apex project."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Renovations / Investment properties"
    title="A clearer plan for your property."
    description="Renovation decisions look different when you are responsible for an investment property. Start with its condition, the next use, and a scope you can make informed decisions about."
    image="/assets/investment-concept.webp"
    alt="AI-generated residential investment property concept with a clean, considered exterior"
  >
    <a class="button button-gold" href="/start-your-project/">Start your project <span aria-hidden="true"><Icon name="arrow-up-right" /></span></a>
  </PageHero>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="investment-priorities">
    <div class="wrap split-content">
      <div class="prose">
        <span class="eyebrow">Property first. Priorities next.</span>
        <h2 id="investment-priorities">Define what this<br />property needs.</h2>
        <p>For North Alabama investment property owners, a useful renovation plan separates condition-related needs from optional cosmetic updates. Tell Apex Property Renovation whether you are preparing a vacant property, planning a turnover, or considering improvements around existing occupancy.</p>
        <p>A project brief might identify worn flooring, interior paint, kitchen or bathroom finishes, storage, or exterior appearance. Include known issues and previous repairs where relevant. Each property or unit needs assessment rather than assumptions based on another renovation.</p>
        <p>For a closer look at room-level decisions, explore <a class="text-link" href="/renovations/kitchens-bathrooms/">kitchen and bathroom renovations</a>. A broader update may benefit from <a class="text-link" href="/renovations/whole-home/">whole-home planning</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <ConceptImage src="/assets/living-concept.webp" alt="AI-generated interior concept showing a restrained material palette and open living space" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-dark" aria-labelledby="investment-decisions">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <span class="eyebrow">Keep the decisions connected</span>
        <h2 id="investment-decisions">Practical selections.<br />Clear owner control.</h2>
        <p>The aim is a well-defined scope and coordinated work, not a promise of a particular rent, resale price, or return.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-grid">
        <article class="feature-card">
          <span class="number">01</span>
          <h3>Unit-level priorities</h3>
          <p>List essential items separately from discretionary improvements. Note occupancy, access arrangements, and any desired turnover date so constraints are visible at the start.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature-card">
          <span class="number">02</span>
          <h3>Durable materials</h3>
          <p>Compare cleanability, repairability, and replacement availability alongside upfront cost. Consistent selections may simplify future maintenance, but suitability still depends on each space.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature-card">
          <span class="number">03</span>
          <h3>Owner visibility</h3>
          <p>Agree on who approves selections and scope changes. A clear record of questions, decisions, and next steps helps keep the property owner's priorities central.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="investment-faq">
    <div class="wrap split-content">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <span class="eyebrow">Investment property questions</span>
        <h2 id="investment-faq">Clarity before commitment.</h2>
        <p>Already an Apex client?</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://apexpropertyportal.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open the client portal <span aria-hidden="true"><Icon name="arrow-up-right" /></span></a>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-list">
        <details>
          <summary>Can I discuss more than one property?</summary>
          <p>Include the number of properties or units and the broad needs of each in your inquiry. They should not be treated as identical scopes. Condition, access, priorities, and sequencing need to be considered individually before plans are agreed.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can a renovation be planned around a turnover?</summary>
          <p>Share the expected vacancy window and any occupancy commitments. Whether that window is realistic depends on the assessment, work required, product availability, and approvals. A preferred date is an important constraint, not a guaranteed completion date.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How are costs and potential returns evaluated?</summary>
          <p>Renovation pricing requires a property-specific assessment and defined selections. Rental income, resale value, and returns depend on factors beyond the renovation itself. Evaluate those assumptions separately rather than treating a scope or finish choice as a financial guarantee.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTA title="Give your next investment a clear plan." text="Share the property condition, your priorities, and the decisions ahead. Start with the scope, not assumptions." />
</SiteLayout>
